; A PARAMETERISED type answered through a bare `Int`.
;
; `Int` is this language's universal heap handle and the whole tree
; relies on it: `mkSpan` declares `Int` and answers a `Span`,
; `format.mkFPr` answers an `FPr`, a struct. `tyReprClash` then names
; only `Bool` and `Float` as representation clashes - a 2026-08-10
; attempt at the general rule reported 21 of 271 files, every one a
; handle returned through `Int` on purpose.
;
; A PARAMETERISED constructor is different in kind. The handle keeps
; the address and throws the type ARGUMENTS away, so nothing
; downstream can recover what it holds and there is nothing left for
; the convention to mean.
;
; Measured twice on 2026-08-26, both silent, both during the `Result`
; migration: `IO.makeDir` declared `Int` over a body that had become
; `sysMkdir`'s `Result` and returned a HEAP ADDRESS where an errno
; belonged - `check` printed OK and a fixture caught it at
; `got=4372103456 want=0`. `driver.runTool` did the same one layer up
; and surfaced as `AX4003` at run time.
;
; The monomorphic handle below is the control: it must stay accepted,
; because that is the convention this rule is carved around.
(import Err)

(struct Span (a : Int) (b : Int))

; The control: a monomorphic handle through `Int`, which is the
; language's convention and is NOT refused.
(:: mkSpan (-> Int Int Int))

(fn (mkSpan a b) (Span a b))

; The defect: a parameterised type through `Int`.
(:: leak (-> Int Int))

(fn (leak x) (Ok x))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) (mkSpan 1 2))
